# CLAUDE.md.ref — capitalisman Project Instructions
<!-- Project-specific instructions. Loaded by simplex_mind via projects.yaml -->
<!-- Agnostic protocols (memory, tickets, git, response summary) live in simplex_mind's CLAUDE.md -->

## Project Overview

**Capitalisman**: a stock & crypto research dashboard. Technical and cross-asset signals are
combined into BUY/SELL/HOLD research calls, with agreement and evidence scores, backtesting,
a step-through historical simulator and a research suite.

- **Stack:** Python + Streamlit. Data via yfinance; indicators via `ta`; plotly charts;
  pandas/numpy/scipy/pyarrow. Optional TimesFM ML forecasting in a separate environment.
- **Purpose:** Personal market-research tool: signal exploration, screening, backtesting.
  BUY/SELL/HOLD are research outputs, never orders; there is no brokerage execution.
- **Provenance:** Fork of a friend's project. `origin` = simplex5/Capitalisman (ours),
  `upstream` = NicholasCStanley/Capitalisman (friend's original, pull-only).
- **User:** simplex5 (single user, local machine only).

---

## Upstream agent files (not instructions for simplex_mind sessions)

The friend's repo ships `AGENTS.md`: his instructions for an agent launched inside this repo.
A simplex_mind session does not follow it: its workflow (tickets, asking, one step at a time,
reply format, commits, pushes) comes from simplex_mind only. The project facts it holds are
copied into this file (How to Operate, Guardrails); this file is the only project instruction file.

- Never edit `AGENTS.md` here: it is upstream's, and every upstream merge would conflict. When an
  upstream merge changes it, copy any new project fact into this file.
- Upstream's `CLAUDE.md` (it only imported `@AGENTS.md`) is deleted here on purpose, so Claude Code
  never loads upstream's instructions. If an upstream merge reports a modify/delete conflict on
  `CLAUDE.md`, keep it deleted (`git rm CLAUDE.md`).
- `docs/AGENT_OPERATIONS.md`, `docs/RESEARCH_WORKFLOW.md` and `docs/TIMESFM.md` are reference for
  the exact arguments, outputs and failure handling of a command named below. Read them for that;
  their workflow wording (when to ask, how to hand off) does not apply.

